narrative:
## Avoiding Memory That Never Frees

XIOM prevents most memory leaks through ownership and drop. But certain patterns — like reference cycles with Rc/Arc — can cause leaks even in safe code.

### What is a Memory Leak?

Memory that's allocated but never freed. The program's memory usage grows indefinitely.

### How XIOM Prevents Leaks

```xiom
fn no_leak() -> Unit {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  data.push(2);
  // data automatically dropped — no leak
}
```

Automatic drop handles 99% of cases.

### The Rc/Arc Cycle Problem

```xiom
pub type Node = {
  value: Int;
  next: Option[Rc[Node]];
}

// Two nodes pointing to each other = cycle
// let a = Rc.new(Node{ value: 1, next: None });
// let b = Rc.new(Node{ value: 2, next: Some(a.clone()) });
// a.next = Some(b.clone());  // CYCLE — memory leaks!
// Both ref_counts never reach 0
```

### Preventing Cycles with Weak

```xiom
pub type TreeNode = {
  value: Int;
  parent: Option<Weak[TreeNode]>,  // weak reference — breaks cycle
  children: Vec[Rc[TreeNode]],
}

// Parent → Child = Rc (strong, keeps alive)
// Child → Parent = Weak (doesn't keep alive)
// No cycle — all memory freed when last strong Rc drops
```

### Tracking Leaks

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let rc = Rc.new(42);
  io.debug(rc.ref_count());  // monitor reference count
  // If ref_count is unexpectedly high, you may have a leak
  return 0;
}
```

### Other Leak Sources

- Channels with senders that never drop
- Global collections that only grow
- Thread handles that aren't joined
- Callbacks that hold strong references

